# Constants, generics, traits and casts before HIR

HIR and backends should not re-solve the same compile-time questions on every function body. Work that can finish early should finish early.

If a declaration is marked compile-time and its value fully folds, fold it. If a generic call needs a concrete function, request one. If a trait bound needs evidence, resolve it. If a conversion needs a cast, emit an explicit operation.

The marker does the deciding, not the value. `#` is what makes a declaration compile-time. A top-level runtime binding stays runtime even when its value never actually changes.

AST owns that static work.

## Folding constants

`greeting_prefix` begins as source:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
greeting_prefix #= "Hello"
]

After checking and folding, it becomes a **backend-neutral compile-time value**. Exported folded facts copy into the immutable module interface. Consumers never re-parse or re-fold the provider's template or literal.

Constants are compile-time declarations and metadata, not runtime top-level statements. Const records may group fully folded struct or anonymous-record constants as field-access-only compile-time data. They are not runtime values to pass around.

Current typed build configuration values and source `#Config` primitives (the Alpha spelling) enter as ordinary folded constants. Accepted `$config` contracts resolve into the same backend-neutral constant lane; they create no runtime wrapper type and no special HIR node category.

## Static Bool control flow

Stage 4 owns the boundary:

[codeblock, $code("text"):
fully checked authored AST
-> constant evaluation
-> known Bool branch selection
-> commit active generated requests and summaries
-> terminality validation over the active control flow
-> active executable AST
-> HIR
]

Stage 4 specialises ordinary executable work from resolved configuration values:

- both branches of an ordinary `if` remain frontend-valid
- a known `Bool` selects the active executable branch after source discovery
- no declaration, dependency or export topology changes

Stage 4 adds no config-value branch node. HIR never redoes the decision, and Moth source has no platform-conditional source mechanism. Future compiler-owned `$feature` is a separate deferred structural-selection mechanism: it may select source structure before graph and declaration publication, but it is not another evaluator.

